      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine the pork, ginger, light soy sauce, Shaoxing wine, sesame oil, egg, and spring onion in a bowl. Mix gently with your hands until just combined. Do not overwork it."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the mixture into 4 equal portions. Wet your hands. Shape each into a rough ball about 8cm across. They should look loose, not smooth."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a wide pan or dutch oven over medium-high heat. Brown the meatballs on all sides, about 3 to 4 minutes total. They will be fragile at this stage. Use a spoon to baste with oil as you turn them. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In the same pan, add the cabbage pieces. Stir briefly to coat in the fat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the meatballs on top of the cabbage. Add the dark soy, sugar, star anise, and 400ml of water. Bring to the boil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cover and reduce to a very low simmer. Braise for 45 minutes, turning the meatballs once halfway through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste the broth and adjust seasoning. Serve the meatballs in deep bowls on a bed of the braised cabbage with the broth ladled over. Eat with steamed rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Meatballs fall apart during browning",
              "cause": "The mixture was too loose or the pan not hot enough.",
              "fix": "Handle gently and use a very hot pan. A slight crust forms quickly and holds the ball together. Baste rather than move them excessively."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Broth is very thin and watery",
              "cause": "Pork with too little fat releases less collagen during braising.",
              "fix": "Remove the lid and simmer uncovered for 10 minutes to concentrate the broth. Add a small splash of dark soy to deepen the colour."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Lion head meatballs are among the most famous dishes of Yangzhou and the broader Jiangsu region. They appear in two versions: a red-braised version using dark soy and a white-braised version cooked in clear broth with minimal soy. The white version is the more refined of the two and is associated with formal Huaiyang banquet cooking. The dish became widely known outside China partly through its appearance in Chinese diaspora restaurant menus across Southeast Asia."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
